{"from": "site:philippi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-12-philippi-city", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated city at Filippoi, inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2016 as the Archaeological Site of Philippi, is identified with the Macedonian city Acts names; it attests the place, not the stay. Regraded A to B (reviewer 11, architect's titles/offices ruling): the site is A for the place alone - moved to a site attests place edge on place:philippi (the acts/08 site-attests-place model); it is B for the visit claim."}
{"from": "site:philippi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-12-philippi-colony", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavation shows a Roman colonial city — a two-terrace forum on the main road, colonial Latin inscriptions — and sources record the triumviral refounding as Colonia Iulia Augusta Philippensis; the 'the foremost city of the district' reading remains disputed (see claim note)."}
{"from": "site:philippi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-20-strategoi", "grade": "B", "note": "The colony was governed by two military officers, the duumviri, appointed from Rome — the same municipal institution Acts shows judging the case as the strategoi; the Greek-title equivalence is interpretation."}
{"from": "site:via-egnatia", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-11-voyage", "grade": "B", "note": "The Via Egnatia, marked by milestones for 860 km across Macedonia and Thrace, carried the land leg 'from there to Philippi' after the landing at Neapolis; it cannot attest the sea voyage from Troas."}
{"from": "artifact:thyatira-dyers-guild-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-14-lydia-seller-purple", "grade": "B", "note": "Inscriptions of the dyers' guild among Thyatira's ruins confirm the city's purple-dyeing trade in the Roman era — the trade Lydia practiced; they do not name Lydia herself."}
{"from": "artifact:delos-proseuche-stelae", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-13-proseuche-riverside", "grade": "C", "note": "Delian stelae (Samaritan community, 3rd-2nd century BCE) use proseuche for a diaspora 'house of prayer' — the institution-word of Acts 16:13; different island, pre-Christian, so context only. No Philippi proseuchē was excavated."}
{"from": "text:cicero-in-verres-2-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-37-citizen-protection", "grade": "B", "note": "Cicero, In Verres 2.5.66: 'It is a crime to bind a Roman citizen; to scourge him is a wickedness; to put him to death is almost parricide' (2.5.63 'O Porcian law!') — the protection of citizens against degrading punishment that Acts 16:37 invokes, in the same legal tradition (Verres's Sicily, 73-70 BCE)."}
{"from": "text:cicero-in-verres-2-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-22-beaten-with-rods", "grade": "C", "note": "The same oration shows flogging/scourging as the standard degrading punishment magistrates ordered — the practice Philippi's magistrates applied with rods; Sicily, not Philippi, so context."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-kiddushin-3-12", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-03-timothy-circumcised", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Kiddushin 3:12 states the matrilineal principle — where the mother's status governs, the offspring 'is not considered his father's son at all, but has the same status as his mother' — the custom that made Timothy, son of a Greek father, count as Jewish and need circumcision. Redacted c. 200 CE; may not describe the 40s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:digest-48-3-12", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-16-27-jailer-life-forfeit", "grade": "C", "note": "Digest 48.3.12pr (Callistratus): guards who lose custody are themselves brought into danger and can be punished — the discipline that made the Philippi jailer expect death for escaped prisoners; later (compiled 533 CE) and general, as for Acts 12."}
{"from": "site:philippi", "type": "identified_as", "to": "place:philippi", "note": "The UNESCO Archaeological Site of Philippi at modern Filippoi is the ancient city; the identification is secure (walls, forum, theatre, inscriptions)."}
